00:02:53.550 --> 00:02:57.770
So if we zoom in on the
unstructured storage systems,

00:02:57.770 --> 00:03:00.520
you know that's file,
object, and block,

00:03:00.520 --> 00:03:02.510
each are good at solving
different problems

00:03:02.510 --> 00:03:04.910
in sort of different domains.

00:03:04.910 --> 00:03:08.810
If we take a file-centric
view on these domains,

00:03:08.810 --> 00:03:10.940
shared file systems are
critical building blocks

00:03:10.940 --> 00:03:12.510
in enterprise workflows.

00:03:12.510 --> 00:03:15.616
But if you're going to manually
stand up Linux instances

00:03:15.616 --> 00:03:17.240
and build your own
file servers, that's

00:03:17.240 --> 00:03:19.130
complex and time consuming.

00:03:19.130 --> 00:03:21.650
And then you're still in the
business of managing filers,

00:03:21.650 --> 00:03:24.140
keeping them up to
date, and so on.

00:03:24.140 --> 00:03:27.980
You can try object storage for
your workloads that need File.

00:03:27.980 --> 00:03:31.280
But sometimes making
object storage

00:03:31.280 --> 00:03:33.580
try and look like File is--

00:03:33.580 --> 00:03:35.930
let's say, it just makes
us grumpy and kind of

00:03:35.930 --> 00:03:37.060
leave it at that.

00:03:37.060 --> 00:03:39.950
If there's not an impedance
mismatch with your workload,

00:03:39.950 --> 00:03:41.280
sometimes it works really well.

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:43.685
But if there is, then
you need a random I/O,

00:03:43.685 --> 00:03:47.100
then it's kind of a disaster.

00:03:47.100 --> 00:03:50.540
And then finally, we have some
really innovative, persistent

00:03:50.540 --> 00:03:53.240
disk features, which are
our virtual disks such

00:03:53.240 --> 00:03:55.910
that you can mount read-only
the same virtual disk

00:03:55.910 --> 00:03:59.030
on a whole huge number of VMs.

00:03:59.030 --> 00:04:00.520
But it's not writable.

00:04:00.520 --> 00:04:03.170
And so, while persistent disk
is good for a certain kind

00:04:03.170 --> 00:04:07.296
of unstructured data, it's
not awesome for sharing files.

00:04:07.296 --> 00:04:09.170
In fact, that's what
network-attached storage

00:04:09.170 --> 00:04:10.946
was invented for.

00:04:10.946 --> 00:04:12.070
And so that's what this is.

