00:07:10.660 --> 00:07:14.330
the biggest difference between
the two tiers is performance.

00:07:14.330 --> 00:07:17.776
In fact, the only difference
is price and performance.

00:07:17.776 --> 00:07:20.890
So you are the best judge of the
application you have at hand.

00:07:20.890 --> 00:07:25.270
But we have a few suggestions
and recommendations for you.

00:07:25.270 --> 00:07:28.750
If you find that you're
running a read-write, random

00:07:28.750 --> 00:07:31.700
read-write, data-intensive
workload, for example,

00:07:31.700 --> 00:07:35.890
a simulation, then you may want
to opt in for the premium tier

00:07:35.890 --> 00:07:39.790
to tap into the full IOPS
that we can export to you.

00:07:39.790 --> 00:07:43.180
On the other hand, if you
expect that your application is

00:07:43.180 --> 00:07:46.450
more cache friendly, then
you can pay a lot less

00:07:46.450 --> 00:07:48.430
and get away with
the standard tier

00:07:48.430 --> 00:07:51.130
and get the cache-read
performance.

00:07:51.130 --> 00:07:53.470
But as we all know,
performance tuning is an art.

00:07:53.470 --> 00:07:56.530
So you're the best judge of
what your application needs.

00:07:56.530 --> 00:08:01.360
Apart from the trifecta of
IOPS, throughput, and latency,

00:08:01.360 --> 00:08:03.460
and we've shared some
