00:17:11.499 --> 00:17:13.290
So let me talk to you
about media rendering

00:17:13.290 --> 00:17:16.859
as an interesting demo here.

00:17:16.859 --> 00:17:19.800
So a typical process we
see with simulation artists

00:17:19.800 --> 00:17:21.960
is they fire a bunch
of simulations off

00:17:21.960 --> 00:17:22.750
at the same time.

00:17:22.750 --> 00:17:25.440
And then they're going to
compare and contrast and then

00:17:25.440 --> 00:17:26.290
pick the best one.

00:17:26.290 --> 00:17:27.831
And that's the one
then they're going

00:17:27.831 --> 00:17:30.000
to go send to a big,
large-scale render.

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:32.070
To do that
efficiently, they need

00:17:32.070 --> 00:17:33.690
to harness a bunch of GPUs.

00:17:33.690 --> 00:17:36.780
And they need a fast file
system shared in order

00:17:36.780 --> 00:17:41.290
to actually do this and
get the real-time feedback.

00:17:41.290 --> 00:17:43.320
So this is a IOPS.

00:17:43.320 --> 00:17:47.334
And this is a performance
throughput heavy tasks

00:17:47.334 --> 00:17:49.500
because we don't want the
multiple simulations to be

00:17:49.500 --> 00:17:52.830
bogged down just
because one of the sims

00:17:52.830 --> 00:17:55.500
is using up all of
the I/O bandwidth.

00:17:55.500 --> 00:17:59.254
All right, so let's
switch to the demo screen.

00:17:59.254 --> 00:17:59.920
All right, cool.

00:18:03.830 --> 00:18:08.280
So because we offer high
IOPS in the single instance,

00:18:08.280 --> 00:18:11.960
you saw on the earlier slide
up to 30,000 read IOPS,

00:18:11.960 --> 00:18:14.270
this is actually possible
to do with a single instance

00:18:14.270 --> 00:18:15.686
on Filestore, even
though you have

00:18:15.686 --> 00:18:17.720
a lot of simulations
running, kind of spread

00:18:17.720 --> 00:18:18.890
out concurrently.

00:18:18.890 --> 00:18:22.775
In order to demo this, I'm
not going to run a simulation.

00:18:22.775 --> 00:18:25.010
I am not a simulation artist.

00:18:25.010 --> 00:18:29.709
I have absolutely no graphic
design talent whatsoever.

00:18:29.709 --> 00:18:31.250
So what I am going
to do is I'm going

00:18:31.250 --> 00:18:33.620
to show you a synthetic
benchmark running FIO, which

00:18:33.620 --> 00:18:37.040
is a very common tool that's
used to do file system

00:18:37.040 --> 00:18:38.700
performance testing.

00:18:38.700 --> 00:18:43.640
I'm going to do a 50-50
read-write workload that's

00:18:43.640 --> 00:18:44.510
going to test IOPS.

00:18:44.510 --> 00:18:47.390
And so this is a
Filestore Premium instance

00:18:47.390 --> 00:18:51.190
mounted on a single, eight
core VM on the client side

00:18:51.190 --> 00:19:00.010
and running against 4K
random iOS, 50-50 read-write.

00:19:00.010 --> 00:19:02.510
And then what I'm going to show
you behind here-- let's see,

00:19:02.510 --> 00:19:05.890
I don't need that anymore.

00:19:05.890 --> 00:19:08.600
What I'm going to show you is
the metrics from the NFS client

00:19:08.600 --> 00:19:09.410
here.

00:19:09.410 --> 00:19:13.580
And you can see on the top
left, we've got read throughput.

00:19:13.580 --> 00:19:15.370
Top right, we got
write throughput.

00:19:15.370 --> 00:19:18.950
And in the bottom we
have aggregate IOPS

00:19:18.950 --> 00:19:22.450
split into read and write,
stacked on top of each other.

00:19:22.450 --> 00:19:26.030
So just for fun, because
what we're talking about

00:19:26.030 --> 00:19:30.140
is IOPS for this workload, I'm
going to zoom in on IOPS here.

00:19:30.140 --> 00:19:34.310
And while the FIO script
is pushing all this traffic

00:19:34.310 --> 00:19:39.830
back and forth from a single VM,
effectively what we're doing,

00:19:39.830 --> 00:19:41.525
is we're simulating a sim.

00:19:41.525 --> 00:19:46.160
And so it's turtles all
the way down, right?

00:19:46.160 --> 00:19:48.020
All right, yeah,
my attempt at humor

00:19:48.020 --> 00:19:51.920
aside, my jokes to
fill in the time

00:19:51.920 --> 00:19:55.130
while the workload actually
ramps up is pretty funny.

00:19:55.130 --> 00:20:02.310
All right, so what we're seeing
here is the NFS client has--

00:20:02.310 --> 00:20:04.490
this is performance
measured at the NFS client,

00:20:04.490 --> 00:20:06.890
not at the NFS server because
that's what you really

00:20:06.890 --> 00:20:07.640
care about, right?

00:20:07.640 --> 00:20:10.098
You care about what performance
is the client going to see.

00:20:10.098 --> 00:20:14.117
And so the client is a
n1-standard-8 with a Prometheus

00:20:14.117 --> 00:20:16.450
reporting agent installed,
reporting out to a Prometheus

00:20:16.450 --> 00:20:18.470
server, and then
the real-time stats

00:20:18.470 --> 00:20:21.140
being modeled with Grafana.

00:20:21.140 --> 00:20:23.810
And so what you see here
is IOPS, combined IOPS,

00:20:23.810 --> 00:20:29.930
of north of 35,000, so close to
40,000 IOPS for this workload.

00:20:29.930 --> 00:20:30.980
Blue is read.

00:20:30.980 --> 00:20:36.935
And pink-ish-- is that a
pink-- is write workloads.

00:20:36.935 --> 00:20:38.310
I mean, you can
get rid of those.

00:20:38.310 --> 00:20:40.770
You don't need those
little popups there.

00:20:40.770 --> 00:20:43.240
As this workload
load runs, you see

00:20:43.240 --> 00:20:44.660
it's doing a 50-50 read-write.

00:20:44.660 --> 00:20:47.580
And so you'll see about equal--

00:20:47.580 --> 00:20:50.569
over time, you'll see about
equal area under each curve.

00:20:50.569 --> 00:20:52.610
And then they're stacked
so you can see aggregate

00:20:52.610 --> 00:20:57.320
is 40,000, which is pretty good.

00:20:57.320 --> 00:21:01.390
How many people here have
deployed file servers?

00:21:01.390 --> 00:21:05.250
40,000 IOPS a pretty good
number for Cloud file server?

00:21:05.250 --> 00:21:06.740
Yeah, right.

00:21:06.740 --> 00:21:07.460
OK.

00:21:07.460 --> 00:21:11.510
For those of you who haven't
deployed file servers,

00:21:11.510 --> 00:21:13.400
this is a great number.

00:21:13.400 --> 00:21:15.760
[LAUGHTER]

00:21:15.760 --> 00:21:16.790
All right.

00:21:16.790 --> 00:21:22.360
And see, nobody's arguing with
me, so I assume this is good.

00:21:22.360 --> 00:21:24.530
And then something
to look forward to

00:21:24.530 --> 00:21:28.160
is there's kind of two
truisms about storage systems.

00:21:28.160 --> 00:21:29.780
They're never big enough.

00:21:29.780 --> 00:21:32.640
And they always
need to be faster.

00:21:32.640 --> 00:21:34.420
Again, no quibbles there, right?

00:21:34.420 --> 00:21:37.850
And so, I think you can imagine
that, over time, Filestore

00:21:37.850 --> 00:21:41.956
will get, not only
bigger, but also faster.

00:21:41.956 --> 00:21:42.980
Let's see.

00:21:42.980 --> 00:21:45.150
Is there anything else I
want to talk about here?

00:21:45.150 --> 00:21:47.220
I think that's basically it.

