right now.

00:24:22.690 --> 00:24:24.080
You can read it off here.

00:24:24.080 --> 00:24:27.010
Again, based on feedback
and as the product evolves,

00:24:27.010 --> 00:24:29.650
this will only get
richer over time.

00:24:29.650 --> 00:24:31.600
You will have the
ability to both monitor

00:24:31.600 --> 00:24:34.660
and set alerts, which
come standard with many

00:24:34.660 --> 00:24:36.220
of our products in Google.

00:24:36.220 --> 00:24:38.354
Filestore is no different.

00:24:38.354 --> 00:24:39.092
Tad?

00:24:39.092 --> 00:24:39.716
TAD HUNT: Cool.

00:24:43.060 --> 00:24:47.020
During Q&amp;A, if you have a
detailed feature, queries,

00:24:47.020 --> 00:24:50.170
definitely speak up.

00:24:50.170 --> 00:24:53.140
Let me talk a minute about
pricing and availability.

00:24:53.140 --> 00:24:58.490
So at GA, when we go General
Availability in the future,

00:24:58.490 --> 00:25:02.360
we plan to be in
every GCP region.

00:25:02.360 --> 00:25:04.180
But in the meantime,
while were in beta,

00:25:04.180 --> 00:25:07.152
these are the regions that
Filestore is available in

00:25:07.152 --> 00:25:08.440
and the regional pricing.

00:25:08.440 --> 00:25:13.690
And so as you can see, the
Standard tier is roughly $0.20

00:25:13.690 --> 00:25:15.740
a gig per month.

00:25:15.740 --> 00:25:18.821
And that Premium tier is
roughly $0.30 a gig per month.

00:25:18.821 --> 00:25:20.320
A little bit of
regional variability

00:25:20.320 --> 00:25:23.590
there but not too much.

00:25:23.590 --> 00:25:25.897
So Filestore is
billed hourly, right?

00:25:25.897 --> 00:25:27.980
And so, you don't have to
pay for the whole month.

00:25:27.980 --> 00:25:29.438
You only pay, if
you use it, you're

00:25:29.438 --> 00:25:30.790
only billed down to the hour.

00:25:30.790 --> 00:25:34.240
And so you're talking,
like, a ten thousandth

00:25:34.240 --> 00:25:37.720
of a cent per hour or something.

00:25:37.720 --> 00:25:38.906
There we go.

00:25:38.906 --> 00:25:43.120
So on the integration
front, we are already

00:25:43.120 --> 00:25:45.077
integrated with a bunch
of Google products

00:25:45.077 --> 00:25:46.660
and a bunch of partner
products, which

00:25:46.660 --> 00:25:48.580
I'll talk about in a
few minutes or we'll

00:25:48.580 --> 00:25:50.380
talk about in a few minutes.

00:25:50.380 --> 00:25:53.040
On Google side, we're integrated
with a Compute Engine,

00:25:53.040 --> 00:25:56.590
Kubernetes Engine, Deployment
Manager, VPC networking,

00:25:56.590 --> 00:25:57.584
and Stackdriver.

00:25:57.584 --> 00:25:59.250
Some of this we've
already talked about.

00:25:59.250 --> 00:26:01.460
And so we're not going to
drill in there too much.

00:26:01.460 --> 00:26:04.270
I want to say a note
about Compute Engine is

00:26:04.270 --> 00:26:07.390
that if you're running
batched-processed workloads,

00:26:07.390 --> 00:26:09.940
you'll often be using
preemptable VMs, which

00:26:09.940 --> 00:26:11.290
are fully supported.

00:26:11.290 --> 00:26:13.080
And a cool thing
with preemptable VMs,

00:26:13.080 --> 00:26:14.620
right, is they
might get torn down

00:26:14.620 --> 00:26:17.410
and a new one restarted for you.

00:26:17.410 --> 00:26:20.300
But Filestore will actually
work in that VM as well.

00:26:20.300 --> 00:26:23.700
And it'll go down and
back up and everything.

00:26:23.700 --> 00:26:24.900
So that's great.

00:26:24.900 --> 00:26:29.140
Kashi's going to talk to you
about our GKE, our Kubernetes

00:26:29.140 --> 00:26:31.769
engine integrations.

00:26:31.769 --> 00:26:33.310
KASHI VISHWANATH:
So I hope everybody

00:26:33.310 --> 00:26:37.330
got the drift on GKE integration
in this conference, right?

00:26:37.330 --> 00:26:39.572
So Filestore product
is no different.

00:26:39.572 --> 00:26:41.530
We have a couple of ways
of doing that for you.

00:26:41.530 --> 00:26:44.140
There is static provisioning
and dynamic provisioning.

00:26:44.140 --> 00:26:46.960
You can see a sample
code YAML on the right.

00:26:46.960 --> 00:26:48.940
I would personally not
take that and compile.

00:26:48.940 --> 00:26:51.650
But you get the idea.

00:26:51.650 --> 00:26:54.760
The idea is you can use it using
the standard interface volume

00:26:54.760 --> 00:26:55.560
plug-in.

00:26:55.560 --> 00:27:00.170
You create the persistent volume
claim, plug in the fileshare.

00:27:00.170 --> 00:27:03.270
One way to obtain that would be
the demo I showed you earlier.

00:27:03.270 --> 00:27:04.770
Plug that in, and
you're good to go.

00:27:04.770 --> 00:27:07.766
So it should work with
Google Kubernetes Engine.

00:27:07.766 --> 00:27:10.320
We also have the
dynamic provisioning, so

00:27:10.320 --> 00:27:11.590
the next slide.

00:27:11.590 --> 00:27:14.370
And a big shout out
to the GKE team, who

00:27:14.370 --> 00:27:16.450
we've worked with internally.

00:27:16.450 --> 00:27:20.850
Using this, we just released
to GitHub the CSI driver.

00:27:20.850 --> 00:27:22.840
The URL is down there.

00:27:22.840 --> 00:27:25.790
Using that, you can
dynamically create and mount

00:27:25.790 --> 00:27:27.820
these fileshare.

00:27:27.820 --> 00:27:30.550
Your workloads do not have
to know about the existence

00:27:30.550 --> 00:27:32.500
of them a priori.

00:27:32.500 --> 00:27:35.690
Furthermore, you can create
multiple search instances

00:27:35.690 --> 00:27:37.450
and dynamically attach them.

00:27:37.450 --> 00:27:40.900
Again, sample code
is on the right.

00:27:40.900 --> 00:27:43.000
The driver example is all there.

00:27:43.000 --> 00:27:45.580
And we'll have more
documentation coming up front.

00:27:45.580 --> 00:27:50.600
So message is fully
integrated with GKE.

00:27:50.600 --> 00:27:52.260
TAD HUNT: Very cool.

00:27:52.260 --> 00:27:54.910
So continuing on the
integration front,

00:27:54.910 --> 00:27:57.480
one of the common things you
need to do with storage systems

00:27:57.480 --> 00:27:59.980
is the dreaded backup.

00:27:59.980 --> 00:28:02.600
Anyone here had to do backups
for your storage systems

00:28:02.600 --> 00:28:04.940
for your file servers?

00:28:04.940 --> 00:28:06.800
Never fun.

00:28:06.800 --> 00:28:08.540
Not super sexy thing to do.

00:28:08.540 --> 00:28:09.740
But you've got to do it.

00:28:09.740 --> 00:28:10.949
And you got to test restores.

00:28:10.949 --> 00:28:12.323
And you're going
to probably have

00:28:12.323 --> 00:28:14.060
to do actual restores
from time to time.

00:28:14.060 --> 00:28:16.220
So on that front,
chances are you're

00:28:16.220 --> 00:28:18.290
working with some
commercial partners already,

00:28:18.290 --> 00:28:20.390
some commercial vendors
for that already.

00:28:20.390 --> 00:28:23.330
We're announcing already an
integration with Actifio.

00:28:23.330 --> 00:28:25.550
And there's a solutions brief.

00:28:25.550 --> 00:28:28.160
And they're available on the
Cloud Launcher right there.

00:28:28.160 --> 00:28:31.010
And so a really cool
thing about Actifio Sky

00:28:31.010 --> 00:28:32.960
is it enables tiering
of your backup data

00:28:32.960 --> 00:28:35.660
into GCS, into Google
Cloud Storage, where

00:28:35.660 --> 00:28:37.820
you can use Nearline
and Coldline as well

00:28:37.820 --> 00:28:41.000
to further reduce costs there.

00:28:41.000 --> 00:28:44.660
And it enables rewind and
restoration of entire file

00:28:44.660 --> 00:28:46.730
systems or even
just single files

00:28:46.730 --> 00:28:50.300
at different points in time.

00:28:50.300 --> 00:28:53.720
If there's other backup
providers that you use,

00:28:53.720 --> 00:28:55.640
feel free to speak
up during the Q&amp;A.

00:28:55.640 --> 00:29:00.680
And we'll start open the
conversations with them.

00:29:00.680 --> 00:29:04.400
So wrapping it up
and just before we

00:29:04.400 --> 00:29:07.950
go to Q&amp;A, which I think
we've got about 15,

00:29:07.950 --> 00:29:12.320
20 minutes for, it's
actually perfect timing,

00:29:12.320 --> 00:29:15.620
I want to leave you with
one key message here, which

00:29:15.620 --> 00:29:18.800
is Filestore is a
new native experience

00:29:18.800 --> 00:29:22.860
for standing up managed file
servers in Google Cloud.

00:29:22.860 --> 00:29:28.675
And it works with Kubernetes
Engine and Compute Engine.

